      "Evidence": "Long stretches lead with mechanism and procedure \u2014 schism physics, weapon nomenclature, cut-by-cut fight choreography, contract/relay logistics \u2014 before any sensory or emotional hook lands; texture like \u0027the camphor,\u0027 \u0027the warm chip,\u0027 or the lumen mice appears late, sparsely, and is quickly subordinated to plot/exposition (e.g., the Gingerbread House\u0027s uncanny resin colors are noted once and then the scene pivots straight into tactical soldier-assessment).",
      "Fix": "Open scenes by dwelling in one or two strange-beautiful sensory details (the wrong-colored resin, the warm damp credstick, the mice\u0027s cold light) for several beats before the tactical/expository machinery takes over, so allure precedes and outweighs the procedural horror."
    },
    {
      "Key": "open_grounded",
      "Title": "Open with the world doing its work",
      "Status": "fail",
      "Evidence": "The story opens directly with plot-relevant sensory/technical exposition (Kyle detecting a schism at 17-19 hertz, measuring its width, rerouting) rather than a grounded, mundane entry beat; within the first page we\u0027re already into world-jargon and escalating danger with no commute/transaction moment that isn\u0027t laden with plot mechanics.",
      "Fix": "Precede the schism-detection beat with a brief, lower-stakes routine moment (e.g., him buying coffee, navigating ordinary traffic) that lets the reader settle into the world\u0027s textures before the plot machinery (schisms, hertz bands, tracking teams) starts demanding comprehension."
    },
    {
